import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Proposal — a kernel IR as data, and the libs it needs","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/reports/2026-10-27-proposal-kernel-ir-lib.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/reports/2026-10-27-proposal-kernel-ir-lib.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/reports/2026-10-27-proposal-kernel-ir-lib.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="proposal-—-a-kernel-ir-as-data-and-the-libs-it-needs" tabindex="-1">Proposal — a kernel IR as data, and the libs it needs <a class="header-anchor" href="#proposal-—-a-kernel-ir-as-data-and-the-libs-it-needs" aria-label="Permalink to “Proposal — a kernel IR as data, and the libs it needs”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Status:</strong> proposed · follows <code>2026-10-27-proposal-int4-kernel-vocabulary.md</code><strong>Question it answers:</strong> if a kernel body should be transparent (a term, not an opaque <code>triton.jit</code> blob), where does that term live and what carries it?</p><h2 id="the-rule-that-constrains-the-answer" tabindex="-1">The rule that constrains the answer <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rule-that-constrains-the-answer" aria-label="Permalink to “The rule that constrains the answer”">​</a></h2><p>The kernel&#39;s six combinators stay closed. A kernel IR is <strong>not</strong> a new set of kernel combinators — that is exactly the &quot;cost of an open kernel&quot; the kernel paper argues against. It enters the platform the way every other language does: <strong>as a <code>Language</code> (data), interpreted by engines.</strong> So the IR is a language, the search is over its terms, and nothing downstream of the kernel grows.</p><h2 id="where-each-piece-lives" tabindex="-1">Where each piece lives <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-each-piece-lives" aria-label="Permalink to “Where each piece lives”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>artifact</th><th>kind</th><th>home</th><th>why there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>kernel IR ops</strong> (<code>tile_load</code>, <code>unpack_i4</code>, <code>dot</code>, <code>split</code>, <code>stage</code>, <code>reduce</code>, <code>store</code>) declared with grades and costs</td><td>data (<code>Language</code>)</td><td>new <strong><code>lib-kernel-ir</code></strong></td><td>a language is data — <code>kit-prelude-lang</code> pattern</td></tr><tr><td><strong>lowering IR → Triton</strong></td><td>engine</td><td>new <strong><code>compile-triton</code></strong></td><td>the <code>compile-*</code> family already exists (<code>compile-js</code>, <code>compile-llvm</code>, <code>compile-dot</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>running a compiled kernel</strong></td><td>port + adapters</td><td><strong><code>lib-triton</code></strong> (contract) + <strong><code>lib-triton-*</code></strong> (impls)</td><td>the <code>lib-X</code> / <code>lib-X-c</code> split the platform already uses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>schedule terms, cost model, certificate, search</strong></td><td>domain</td><td><strong><code>kernelopt</code></strong></td><td>it already owns <code>Kernel_RULES</code>, the sink, the cost model and the sieve</td></tr><tr><td><strong>deploy-regime referee, routing</strong></td><td>measurement</td><td><strong><code>graphopt</code></strong></td><td><code>bench/benchkit/regime.py</code> lives there</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Nothing new is invented beyond the IR itself; every other artifact lands in a family that already exists.</p><h2 id="the-ir-kept-deliberately-small" tabindex="-1">The IR, kept deliberately small <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-ir-kept-deliberately-small" aria-label="Permalink to “The IR, kept deliberately small”">​</a></h2><p>Ten ops, each with a <strong>denotation</strong> (a torch reference, so equivalence is checkable) and a <strong>cost</strong> (bytes, ops, launches — the enrichment):</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>tile_load   (tensor, idx)        -&gt; tile</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>unpack_i4   (packed, shift)      -&gt; int4 tile</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>dequant     (q, zero, scale)     -&gt; fp tile</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>dot         (a, b)               -&gt; tile</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>split       (k_range, n_parts)   -&gt; k_range</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>stage       (partial)            -&gt; partial</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>reduce      (partials)           -&gt; tile</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>store       (tile, idx)          -&gt; ()</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>bias        (tile, bias)         -&gt; tile</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The test of &quot;is the body transparent&quot; is then mechanical: two terms are equal iff their denotations are equal. <code>split_k = 4</code> becomes four <code>split</code> terms with provably equal composed denotation, not a parameter someone hopes is safe.</p><h2 id="milestones" tabindex="-1">Milestones <a class="header-anchor" href="#milestones" aria-label="Permalink to “Milestones”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>deliverable</th><th>gate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>M0</strong></td><td>IR declared in <code>lib-kernel-ir</code>; denotation + cost per op</td><td>every op has a torch reference and a byte count</td></tr><tr><td><strong>M1</strong></td><td><code>compile-triton</code> lowers the four <code>split_k</code> variants</td><td>all four denotations equal to the incumbent&#39;s (cosine ≥ 0.9999)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>M2</strong></td><td><code>kernelopt</code> searches the IR for <code>down_proj</code></td><td>matches 2.96× or beats 42 % of roofline, structure not named by hand</td></tr><tr><td><strong>M3</strong></td><td>add <code>tile_load</code> with M&gt;1</td><td>covers prefill and <code>gate_proj</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="review-—-what-is-wrong-with-the-above" tabindex="-1">Review — what is wrong with the above <a class="header-anchor" href="#review-—-what-is-wrong-with-the-above" aria-label="Permalink to “Review — what is wrong with the above”">​</a></h2><p><strong>1. The IR as specified cannot express what makes kernels fast.</strong> The op list has <code>tile_load</code>, <code>dot</code>, <code>split</code>, <code>stage</code>, <code>reduce</code> — all <em>data</em> operations. It has no software pipelining, no accumulator reuse across K tiles, no shared-memory staging. Those are the things that separate 42 % of roofline from 70 %. A schedule language that cannot state them can only express naive schedules, which will lose to the hand kernel every time. <strong>Minimum additions:</strong> a <code>pipeline(depth)</code> combinator and <code>stage_shared</code> (a residency annotation), or the IR is decorative.</p><p><strong>2. Denotation equality proves meaning, not schedule equivalence.</strong> Two terms with identical denotations can differ 3× in cost — that is the whole point of searching. So &quot;certify the body&quot; reduces to the law we already have; the IR&#39;s real payoff is <em>search</em>, and the review should say so instead of implying it buys proof.</p><p><strong>3. A static cost model will prune winners.</strong> Evidence from this week: the grid search&#39;s own warm-cache ranking contradicted the deploy-regime ranking on three of four shapes. A cost model is a cruder ranking than a measurement, so it must be used only as a <strong>hard bound</strong> (roofline lower bound, legality), never to choose between feasible candidates.</p><p><strong>4. Search is rate-limited by Triton compilation, not by ideas.</strong> 288 candidates ≈ minutes; 10⁴ candidates ≈ hours of compilation. The proposal does not mention a compile cache or a way to instantiate without a full <code>triton.jit</code> round trip. Without that, the IR cannot be searched at the scale where it beats hand-writing.</p><p><strong>5. The economics are unstated.</strong> Today&#39;s result — 29 % end-to-end — came from a hand-written kernel plus a config sweep. The IR is justified <em>only</em> if it then covers many shapes and formats. If the plan is &quot;a few more kernels&quot;, the IR is over-engineering; if the plan is &quot;hundreds of shapes across GPTQ/AWQ/FP8&quot;, it is the only thing that scales. That decision should be explicit before M0.</p><p><strong>6. Legitimacy, not just design.</strong> A new <code>Language</code> in the platform needs an ADR in the host repo — the kernel stays closed and the IR enters as data, which is the right shape, but it should be recorded as such or the next proposal will quietly add a combinator.</p><h3 id="recommended-cut-down-for-m1" tabindex="-1">Recommended cut-down for M1 <a class="header-anchor" href="#recommended-cut-down-for-m1" aria-label="Permalink to “Recommended cut-down for M1”">​</a></h3><p>Do <strong>not</strong> build the ten-op IR first. Build <strong>one op end to end</strong>: <code>qgemv</code> with a fixed lowering, its torch denotation, and the four <code>split_k</code> variants. Gate: all four denotations equal to <code>awq_gemm</code> (cosine ≥ 0.9999) <em>and</em> at least one variant matching the hand kernel&#39;s speed.</p><p>If that works, the IR earns its second op. If the fixed lowering cannot match a hand-written kernel on one op, generalising it is wasted work.</p><ul><li><strong>IR creep.</strong> Ten ops is the budget; an IR that grows into a compiler project loses to just writing Triton. Anything that cannot be expressed in these ten is a finding about the platform.</li><li><strong>Lowering quality.</strong> <code>compile-triton</code> must reach the same code quality as the hand kernel, or the IR has bought provability at the cost of speed — measured, not assumed.</li><li><strong>Residual opacity, stated once:</strong> the <em>silicon&#39;s response</em> (registers, bank conflicts, ptxas) is never derivable from a term. Cost stays model + measurement. That is why the referee is part of the design and not an afterthought.</li></ul>`,24)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};